From BobBaskin at westat.com  Tue Feb  5 19:02:16 2002
From: BobBaskin at westat.com (Bob Baskin)
Date: Tue, 05 Feb 2002 14:02:16 -0500
Subject: Re: memory and randomization
Message-ID: <15104802790BD411A2C100D0B73EA33C02DED505@remailnt3-re01.westat.com>

some good thoughts here by erin.  

i do have a question about the original post:
<I used 2 loops one with 1000 iterations ( this loop is used to get new data
at every time , new dependent variable and independent variables
"simulation")
the inside loop with 100 iterations (this loop contains all randomization 
approaches ,bootstrap and jackknife).>

first, within your 1000 iteration loop you generate a new dataset, say x.

now, i am somewhat puzzled by what you are doing with the jackknife inside
the inner loop.

are you recalculating the jackknife 100 times on x in the inner loop?? (if
you are - try taking the jackknife out of the inner loop)

are you using the inner 100 iteration loop to calculate the individual
delete-k versions of the statistic by hand?  if so, try using the built-in
jackknife and you won't need the inner loop (go to help and type jackknife).
if you want to roll-your-own, then vectorize it.

for the bootstrap:
are you recalculating an actual bootstrap estimate 100 times and taking the
mean/media/other or are you running a roll-your-own version of the
bootstrap?
you might try using the built-in bootstrap without an inner loop.  if you
are using the built-in bootstrap 100 times what kind of variance are you
trying to simulate?

the only way i can think of, that you really need an inner loop, is if you
are doing some unusual version of the bootstrap such as a sequential
bootstrap.

the best thing to do with a simulation loop is not to nest it unless you are
forced to.

good luck
bob

