A colleague is trying to use the lines command within a trellis panel
function without much luck.  I unsuccessfully tried to help before finally
offering to email his question to this list. 

Here is the scenario:  He is plotting sensitivity (variable 'se') vs.
1-specificity (variable 'fprate) from a variety of studies.  There are two
types of studies he wishes to condition on (Types L and F defined by the
'Setting' variable).

Finally, some studies offer two cutoffs so they provide two pairs of
sensitivity and 1-specificity values.

The trellis plot as shown below plots the points just fine.  He would like
to connect the points from the same study with a line segment, however,
and this is where the plot behaves unexpectedly.

He creates the variable 'studies2' which identifies the Study numbers that
offer two pairs of sensitivity/1-specificity values.  Then in the for
loop, uses the lines command to try to connect each pair of points.  The
lines that are drawn are incorrect, however.

For instance lines start and end where there are no points.  It seems that
the values from conditioning on Settings may not be reaching the lines
command as expected. 

If any knows what we are doing wrong, please share your ideas with us.

Would you please include Peter Imrey in any replies.

Thank you very much.

Jason

###################

# se: vector of sensitivities
# fprate: vector of 1-specificities
# Setting: factor variable containing two unique levels.
# MyDSNum: vector of study IDs.  Some studies provide one observation,
#   some provide two.
# pts: vector of 1s and 2s detailing how many obs this each study offers

xyplot(se~fprate|Setting,data=irvinesespplus,xlab="False Positive 
Rate",ylab="Sensitivity",z=MyDSNum,ylim=c(-0.02,1.02),
panel = function(x,y,z,...) {
        studies<-unique(z);  studies2<-unique(z[pts==2])
        panel.xyplot(jitter(x[studies]),jitter(y[studies]),col=1,pch=16,...)
        lines(c(0,1),c(0,1),lwd=2)
        for (i in 1:length(studies2)){
        lines(x[z==studies2[i]],y[z==studies2[i]])
                }       
        }
)

