From P.J.Wells at open.ac.uk  Wed Feb  6 16:36:24 2002
From: P.J.Wells at open.ac.uk
Date: Wed, 06 Feb 2002 16:36:24 +0000
Subject: Re: Another newbie question about computation time
Message-ID: <F9D44AC0D89CD311B9E3000629385C40044D9E66@marconi.open.ac.uk>

Many thanks to those who replied.

Prof Ripley and Michael Prager both suggested cutting and pasting from the
script window to the commands window, which seems to do the business --
about 12 minutes for each of four iterations of

thingN <- foo(xN, yN)
dump.data("thingN", "D:\\folder\\thingN")
rm(thingN)

To quote Michael's reply, the explanation is that:

>Memory management in S+ is idiosyncratic. It saves all kinds of 
>intermediate results in case it needs to back out of an incomplete 
>evaluation. A script is considered a single evaluation and causes that 
>problem. It is claimed that S+ 6 works better in this regard.

Prof Ripley points out -- very usefully: it might have been the next thing I
tried -- that submitting from a script window implicitly uses source(), and
that using this explicitly would encounter the same difficulties; instead
bear in mind that:

>The only ways that avoid this slow-down with a script are to 
>use splus.exe
>/BATCH or sqpe.exe: see `S Programming' for fuller details.  
>But pasting
>into the command window is usually also effective.

Michael's explanation was on the lines of what I had been expecting (purely
on the basis of a memory that I had seen similar points made in previous
posts), and the following further contributions bear on this:

James Holtman helpfully provided a reminder that one can monitor one's
system's use of resources under Windows using the built-in performance
monitor.

James Janisse pointed out that one could get into trouble with inappropriate
combinations of versions of S+ and Windows. He reported that successive
simulations, which took equal amounts of time when run in S+ 2k under Win98,
took progressively longer times after upgrading from a 600 MHz Pentium to a
1.9GHz with Win2k. Upgrading to S+ 6 made the problem go away.

Now I have some further questions:

1) to Michael: you wrote

> write the script, but do not run it from the script 
>window. Instead, select all the text (Ctrl-A) and copy it to the Windows 
>clipboard (Ctrl-C).  Then make the S-Plus commands window active and paste 
>in the program (Ctrl-V).

In fact, as implied above, I went direct from script to command window. Was
there any particular reason for your proposal, or was it just what came to
mind at the time?

2) I thought that introducing 

dump.data("thingN", "D:\\folder\\thingN")
rm(thingN)

would obviate the problem of S+ hanging on to intermediate output. As it
turned out, it was a palliative rather than a complete solution.

But why? Presumably multiple instances of

thingN <- foo(xN, yN)

alone are causing S+ to cling to more stuff than when each object is removed
after data.dump() -- which in the case of foo() is 10^5 numbers plus the
associated L-moment calculations mentioned: rather a lot...

What is being retained by successive

thingN <- foo(xN, yN)
dump.data("thingN", "D:\\folder\\thingN")
rm(thingN)

My guess is "copies of each thingN" (which saved as files come to about 167k
each). Is this right?

