Ray,

 

A quick follow up 
to clarify, as pointed out to me by Joseph Lucke:

 

I failed to 
differentiate between the math versus the software code.  My 
apologies.

 

The NA is the 
result of the inability from a software perspective to represent the result, 
since it is "undefined" when x=0.  If you review the component steps 
when x=0, you get:

 

> 
log(0)
[1] -Inf

> 
0*-Inf
[1] NA

In your code, you 
should include a check for x>0 and explicitly define the result as 
0 when x <= 0, if that is result you wish to achieve.  Curiously, in the 
help for "ifelse", you find the following example code:

 

log(ifelse(x>0, x, NA))

 

Spin that 
around and you get:

 

x * 
ifelse(x>0, log(x), 0)

 

which may help to 
achieve your goal.

 

 

 

Sorry for any 
confusion.  Thanks Joseph.  Time for another cup of coffee...  
;-)

 

Marc

 

From JRoyRobertson at rohmhaas.com  Thu Feb  7 16:07:28 2002
From: JRoyRobertson at rohmhaas.com
Date: Thu, 07 Feb 2002 11:07:28 -0500
Subject: Re: X*log(X)
Message-ID: <OF6A750744.17A40D89-ON85256B59.005687D9@ROHMHAAS.COM>

lim(x*ln(x)) as x goes to 0 = -lim(-ln(x)/(1/x)). Both -ln(x) and 1/x go 
to +infinity so apply l'Hospital's rule.

lim(x*ln(x)) = -lim[-(1/x)/-(1/x^2)]=-lim(x)=0.

Use x*ifelse(x>0,log(x),0), assuming no negative x.

