In S-Plus 6
> x 
<- 0
> log(x)
[1] -Inf
> x * log(x)
[1] NA
> log(x) * 
x
[1] NA

The program calculates the answer in sequential steps, not as 
a limit. It first evaluates log(x) as -Inf, then multiplies -Inf by x and 
obtains NA. To avoid evaluation of log(0) requires some conditional calculation. 
Examples such as ifelse(x > 0, log(x), 0) are in nearly every S 
book.

Tony Plate
This is one of those cases where 
numerical evaluation of an expression in floating point arithmetic will not give 
the correct result: mathematical (symbolic) analysis of the expression is 
required to correctly evaluate the result.

These kinds of cases can occur 
not only where numerical evaluation gives NA, but also where numerical 
evaluation in floating point arithmetic gives an ordinary number, e.g., it's 
easy to see that ((1+1e-40)-1)/1e-40 should be 1, but if you evaluate it in 
almost any floating-point numerical evaluator (such as Splus) you will get 
0.
-- Tony Plate

