JRoyRobertson at rohmhaas.com wrote:

lim(x*ln(x)) as x goes to 0 = 
-lim(-ln(x)/(1/x)). Both -ln(x) and 1/x go to +infinity so apply l'Hospital's 
rule. lim(x*ln(x)) = -lim[-(1/x)/-(1/x^2)]=-lim(x)=0. 

Use 
x*ifelse(x>0,log(x),0), assuming no negative x.
Roy Robertson

Splus 
does not take limits when asked to evaluate an expression. It does take limits 
in some basic functions.  E.g., log(0.0) is -Inf because only the path from 
positive (real) x down to 0 makes sense  and log(0+i0) is NaN because the 
limit doesn't exist in the complex plane -- it depends on the 
path.

However the * function just sees 0 * -Inf in this case, it doesn't 
know how they were related to each other.  One could write a lHospital 
function that worked on the entire expression to find the limit (see the deriv() 
or D() function for how to do this), but the basic evaluator just uses the 
numbers it sees.
Bill 
Dunlap                                      

But doesn't the function below accomplish the same thing?
One could 
use ifelse(x>T,log(x),0) where T is some tolerance level to ccomodate the 
machine precision.

> testlog <- function(x)   
x*ifelse(x>0, log(x), 0) 
> testlog(0)
[1] 0
> 2
[1] 
2
> testlog(2)
[1] 1.386294
> 2*log(2)
[1] 1.386294
