Dear Julian,

At 02:22 PM 2/10/2002 +0000, P.J.Wells at open.ac.uk wrote:
> This is a request for insight, rather than help:
>
>
>
> > lapply(L.stats.mean, unlist)
>
>
>
> produces
>
>
>
> $R.gill.1:
>     L.1   L.cv  tau.3  tau.4  tau.5
>  0.2454 0.5659 0.4032 0.2599 0.1886
>
> $R.gill.2:
>     L.1   L.cv  tau.3  tau.4  tau.5
>  0.2166 0.6087 0.3213 0.3043 0.1183
>
>
> and so on for another 19 items in the list object L.stats.mean, as required.

Right. unlist is applied to each element of L.stats.mean, producing a list 
of vectors from what was (presumably) a list of lists.

> I realise that lapply() is the recommended method, but I'm puzzled about the
> following:
>
> > unlist(L.stats.mean[2])
>
>
>
>  R.gill.2.L.1 R.gill.2.L.cv R.gill.2.tau.3 R.gill.2.tau.4 R.gill.2.tau.5
>        0.2166        0.6087         0.3213         0.3043         0.1183

Here, you're unlisting a one-element list containing the second element of 
L.stats.mean, which was a list. (This will produce the same result as 
unlist(L.stats.mean[[2]]), which unlists the second element of L.stats.mean.)

> and
>
>
>
> > i <- 2
> > unlist(L.stats.mean[i])
>  R.gill.2.L.1 R.gill.2.L.cv R.gill.2.tau.3 R.gill.2.tau.4 R.gill.2.tau.5
>        0.2166        0.6087         0.3213         0.3043         0.1183

This is identical to the previous example.

> but
>
>
>
> > for(i in 1:21)
>         unlist(L.stats.mean[i])
>
> results in nothing, not even an error or warning message. Clearly I'm
> missing something, but what?

That's because you're not doing anything with the result of each iteration, 
and loops don't return a useful result [see help('for')]. You could try 
something like

        result <- L.stats.mean
        for (i in 1:21) result[[i]] <- unlist(result[i])
        result

This should work but wouldn't be a desirable way to proceed.

> If it makes a difference, the above is using S+ 2k Professional.

The version doesn't matter, as far as I know.

Does that help?
  John

