From gardar at stat.ohio-state.edu  Mon Feb 11 17:39:12 2002
From: gardar at stat.ohio-state.edu (Gardar Johannesson)
Date: Mon, 11 Feb 2002 12:39:12 -0500
Subject: Re: for-loop and memory
In-Reply-To: <20020211110854.1f6a75b3.fharrell@virginia.edu>
References: <20020211110854.1f6a75b3.fharrell@virginia.edu>
Message-ID: <Pine.SOL.4.33.0202111225060.23298-100000@spatial.stat.ohio-state.edu>

It often helps to create a single function that does all the loop-job, for
example, do:

n.draw <- 107
lr <- 20.233
p0 <- 1/6
n.rep <- 15000
lr.res <- numeric(n.rep)
get.lr <- function(p0,n.draw) {
  draw.i <- floor(1+6*runif(n.draw))
  freq.i <- table(draw.i)
  p.i <- freq.i/n.draw
  2*sum(freq.i*log(p.i/p0))
}
print(date())
for(i in 1:n.rep)
  lr.res[i] <- get.lr(p0,n.draw)
print(date())
sum(lr.res > lr)

Doing this ran both slightly faster and took less memory for me (about 3m
on a 450MHz super-sparc II runing Solaris).  When running the original
code, S-plus 6.0 started grapping 43Mb/12Mb (SIZE=43, RES=12) and ended 3m
later in 61Mb/30Mb.  The code above ended in 45Mb/15Mb 2.8m later.
Increasing n.rep to 30000 ended in 48Mb/18Mb 5.9m later...

Good luck, Gardar

ps. R-1.4.1 was both faster and took less memory :)

