Subject: modelling an exponential asymptotic relationship
Message-ID: <00d101c1b0c1$23c7d500$e5656f83@zoo.cam.ac.uk>

I have Splus 2000 professional. I am a PhD zoology student without a strong
mathematical background so please let me know if some of this doesn't make
sense.

I am trying to fit a model based on an exponential asymptotic relationship:
density = exp( A - B * exp( -C * x ))
where A, B and C are the parameters I am trying to estimate:
A = asymptote, B = difference between the asymptote and the intercept, C =
slope.
Therefore, I will need to carry out a non-linear analysis.

My y variable is count data and I am assuming poisson errors so in order to
model density as a function of the x variable I need to use area as an
offset:

count = area * exp( A - B * exp( -C * x ))

I need to run a model in Splus that will give the values of parameters A, B
and C that minimise the poisson deviance and hence these will be maximum
likelihood estimations.
I think the poisson deviance that I am trying to minimise should be:
-2 * ( -area * exp( A - B * exp( -C * x )) + count * ln( area * exp( A - B *
exp( -C * x ))) - count! )

Please can someone let me know how to do this? Please bear in mind that I
have 150,000 rows of data for some of my datasets. It looks as if one ought
to be able to minimise the deviance using the ms() function but if so, I'm
