From lambert.winifred at ensco.com  Tue Feb 12 14:03:35 2002
From: lambert.winifred at ensco.com (Lambert.Winnie)
Date: Tue, 12 Feb 2002 09:03:35 -0500
Subject: Summary: Dual processors
Message-ID: <8986151694190742869D08450EE4DCDE08EC88@amu-exch.ensco.win>

Wow - there were a lot of responses on this one.   The important excerpts from 
each email are given below.  What I've gleaned from the information is that 
S-PLUS will not use multiple processors unless it is doing matrix calculations 
(see Yeng Bun's response).  It's good to have dual processors, though.  One for 
S-PLUS and one for everything else.  If you have questions on someone's 
response, contact that person for info.  Thanks to everyone.

Dave Homiak:  "We have seen no improvement with either version on an HP 8-way 
server, so I would say it does not appear to be multi-threaded ... you can use 
the /MULTIPLEINSTANCES command-line switch to run a copy of S-Plus on each 
available processor simultaneously, and reduce the total computational time at 
the expense of using more memory."

George Watters:  "...I have no "hard" data re: processing times etc., just a 
feeling from use. I used Splus 2000 on a dual processor PC since about the time 
the software was released.  My experience was that the dual processor was nice 
when you want to have an Splus process running and simultaneously do stuff like 
work with your word processor or spreadsheet.  If all you want to do is Splus, 
with no other applications running, then I'm not sure you would get a big 
performance boost.  The "best" way to get a performance increase for Splus is 
to invest in as much RAM as you can afford.  I also think you get snappier 
performance if there is a good amount of RAM on the graphics card.  This seems 
to be particularly true if you want to have Splus running in background to a 
word processor or whatever and periodically switch back to Splus to monitor 
progress.  When the graphics card is slow your ability to monitor progress also 
seems slow."

Kim Elmore:  "I'm using SP2000 on a dual 733 P-III box under Windows 2000.  I 
like 
it.  Things *are* faster, but not because S-Plus knows anything about 
parallel processing.  Rather, it's because S-Plus effectively gets an 
entire processor all to itself.  Any additional tasks, OS, word processing, 
etc., are handled by the other processor.  This is especially true if you 
bump up the execution priority for S-Plus."

Yeng Bun:  "By default, S+6 for Windows uses all processors to perform matrix 
computations.  The matrix computations are based on the Intel Math Kernel 
library (MKL). ... To use only one processor, one needs to restart S+ and set 
the preference via intelmkl.use():
> intelmkl.use(T, number.of.processors=1)
[1] T
> sys.time(a %*% b)
[1] 3.953 3.953"

Chuck Taylor:  "S-PLUS 2000 and S-PLUS 6 for Windows both have the ability to 
use multiple processors for certain kinds of matrix computations. Please see 
the discussion on "Improving Speed" in Chapter 21, "Using Less Time and 
Memory", in the S-PLUS 6 for Windows Programmer's Guide."

James Holtman:  "I don't think that your Splus application will run any faster 
on a 2-CPU system.  Splus is single threaded and if your application is compute 
bound (like most of them are), it will only run on a single CPU. ..."

Prof. Ripley:  "...No increase: S-PLUS is single-threaded: many Windows 
packages are not."

Gerry Blais:  "I have a two-processor machine, and haven't seen any difference 
(but then, I haven't looked very hard, either.)  As far as I know, Windows 
won't/can't help an application that isn't written to take advantage of 
multiprocessors."

Vadim Kutsyy:  "I am running Splus on dual windows and linux (PIII and XEON).  
Splus does not utilize dual processors, but M$ Windows kernel is huge and not 
optimized, so under Windows, dual processor does help, by putting all M$ 
overhead on separate processor.  Linux kernel is optimized, so overhead is 
minimum."

John Fennick:  "I have been using dual processor machines under Win NT, and Win 
2000, with S-Plus 2000 and now version 6 for a few years now. ... if you do a 
great deal of matrix manipulation, I do believe it is noticeably faster.  And, 
side benefits, non-S-plus activities are really better. Background things, 
downloads, any true multi-tasking, is much smoother and faster. I would not go 
back to the old way."

Wils Corrigan:  "I've been running S-Plus 2000 on a dual-processor machine for 
several years now.  The machine is a dual-processor 200 MHz Pentium Pro with 
448 MB RAM running WindowsNT.  It's a little slow compared to current computers 
but it allows you to actually multi-task while using Windows.  I have never 
seen any benefit to S-Plus itself because of the dual-processors. ... I would 
recommend a dual-processor machine if you run more than one number-crunching 
application at a time, or even just like to get other work done while something 
long is running."

Phillip Stafford:  "Look at intelmkl.use(set=T, number.of.processors=1 or 2) 
command.  I have a dual 933 Xeon 2G machine from Dell (precision 620) and the 
major benefit is running multiple instances ("C:\Program 
Files\Insightful\splus6\cmd\SPLUS.exe" /MULTIPLEINSTANCES) of S+ at the same 
time, other than that, though, it's a wash."

