Subject: NA's messing with step()
Message-ID: <3C694BBB.AC78D295@pdf.com>

Hi Splus colleagues,
I was working with step() earlier and noticed that the known significant
terms are not being picked up and I was getting the null model back. 
Investigating further, I think the problem is in the treatment of NA's
in step().  If I pass along a data without NA's: everything works
fine.  If I pass a data with NA's: problem.
Here is an illustration:
> df <- data.frame(y=runif(50)^2,x1=1:50,x2=ifelse(runif(50)<.05,NA,runif(50)))
>
> df$x3 <- df$y+runif(50)
>
> out4 <- lm(y~1, df, na.action="">
>
> out.sw4 <- step(out4, ~x1+x2+x3, trace=T)
>
> out5 <- lm(y~1, df[!is.na(df$x2),], na.action="">
>
> out.sw5 <- step(out5, ~x1+x2+x3, trace=T)
>
>
> ### OUTPUT
>
> > out.sw4
>
> Call:
>
> lm(formula = y ~ 1, data = "" na.action = "">

> Coefficients:
>
>  (Intercept)
>
>    0.3820824
> Degrees of freedom: 50 total; 49 residual
>
> Residual standard error: 0.3117443
>
> > out.sw5
>
> Call:
>
> lm(formula = y ~ x3, data = "" ], na.action
> = "">
> Coefficients:
>
>  (Intercept)        x3
>
>  -0.08426387 0.5441242
> Degrees of freedom: 48 total; 46 residual
>
> Residual standard error (on weighted scale): 0.2190003
>
>

Please note the "out.sw4" contained only the intersect term.  And,
yes, there were out.sw1, out.sw2, ... from checking on other hypotheses.
Has anyone else experienced this problem?
Also, would some kind soul suggest a good read or two on how to deal
with missing data in general?
BTW, I'm using Splus 2000 but I also confirm this peculiarity in Splus
6.
 - Paul

 

 

