From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Tue, 12 Feb 2002 17:25:36 +0000
Subject: Re: NA's messing with step()
In-Reply-To: <3C694BBB.AC78D295@pdf.com>
References: <3C694BBB.AC78D295@pdf.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.44.0202121722190.23824-100000@auk.stats>

On Tue, 12 Feb 2002, Paul Lin wrote:

> Hi Splus colleagues,
>
> I was working with step() earlier and noticed that the known significant
> terms are not being picked up and I was getting the null model back.
> Investigating further, I think the problem is in the treatment of NA's
> in step().  If I pass along a data without NA's: everything works fine.
> If I pass a data with NA's: problem.

[...]

> Has anyone else experienced this problem?

Yes, at least my students have fallen foul of it.

> Also, would some kind soul suggest a good read or two on how to deal
> with missing data in general?

In this case, use na.omit() on your data frame first.  step does not
take NAs into account.

stepAIC (which is safer in many ways) does tell you the problem here
and refuses to give wrong answers.

