(1)  You may already know this, but have you considered
Matlab or Mathematica instead (actually, I think Mathematica's
the one you want for analytical stuff)?  
S-plus does alot, but it's not designed for analytical solutions.  
But it's easy to write code for  numerical approximations and 
Monte Carlo estimates.  Even then, those might be better done in 
Matlab also.  -Iyue Sung [ISung at neri.org]

(2) nlmin(), nlminb()...and related functions will find minima and maxima
for you.  I've programmed quite a bit of my own calc tricks in S, but this
should get you started. I think Newton Raphson is
the default with other options to choose from.  However, it is true that S
is certainly not Mathematica, or Maple--symbolic
languages.  -Erin Kammann [Kammann_Erin at Alkermes.com]

(3) you might check out the following in the help function:
solve (solves linear equations)
Ordinary Differential Equations (also see: deriv)
Mathematical Operations
integrate (links to quadpack)
polyroot (root of polynomial)

You can also link c/c++ or fortran that does numerical root finding,
integration, differetiation, etc.  
