From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Fri Feb 15 11:26:07 2002
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Fri, 15 Feb 2002 11:26:07 +0000
Subject: Re: factor coef's with GAMs
In-Reply-To: <20020215105441.9C5402235@pobox.une.edu.au>
References: <20020215105441.9C5402235@pobox.une.edu.au>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.31.0202151115270.3933-100000@gannet.stats>

On Fri, 15 Feb 2002, Mal Ridges wrote:

> Forgive me if this is an obvious question...but I am new to S-Plus.
>
> I am including a factor variable along with several continuous
> ones in a gam model, with the contrasts set as default. My question
> concerns the coeficients returned, and to which levels of the factor
> each relates to. The output of the model only numbers each coeficient,
> not indicating which one it relates to. As there are k-1 coeficients
> I assume the first coeficient will equal zero, but which one is it?

By default, none of them, as Helmert contrast encoding is used.
So you are seeing the coefficients of k-1 contrasts.  The first contrasts
level 2 with level 1, the second level 3 with levels 1 and 2, and so on.

I suggest that if this is a glm (and not a Gaussian gam) you use

option(contrasts=c("contr.treatment", "contr.poly"))

Then the first factor level is the one omitted.  But in general trying to
interpret coefficients is a not a good idea: it is easier and safer to
interpret predictions.

> I suspect that it is ordered alphanumericaly using the labels of each level,
> but I can't find any documentation that explains whether this is so. What
> concerns me is that if I recode one level, all the coeficients change,
> although
> the intercept remains the same.

Ordering is by the factor levels, which is by default lexicographic
in the ASCII encoding, but need not be,

It's a little strange that the intercept remains the same: that suggests
you are using a Gaussian model or have a lot of balance.  (That's the
reason for using a contrast-based encoding in the Gaussian case.)

> Any help appreciated.

The only place I know this is explained in any detail is in Venables &
Ripley `Modern Applied Statistics with S-PLUS'.

